WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:12.526
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:13.366 --> 00:00:14.347
My name is Alex.

3
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:20.631
Mr. Fay just told us about the tallest mountain on Earth—Mount Everest.

4
00:00:21.591 --> 00:00:26.414
I can't wait to tell you about it because this lesson changed my life!

5
00:00:27.275 --> 00:00:28.796
But more about that later.

6
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.624
Mount Everest is part of the Himalayas, a huge mountain range in Asia.

7
00:00:35.465 --> 00:00:41.690
The Himalayas are known as the roof of the world because many of the world's biggest mountains are

8
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:49.677
here. Mount Everest, the tallest of them all, is about 8,848 meters high.

9
00:00:50.658 --> 00:00:53.320
Its summit is the highest point on Earth!

10
00:00:54.361 --> 00:00:57.744
Mount Everest is shaped like a three-sided pyramid.

11
00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:01.694
Two of these sides face Tibet, near China.

12
00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:05.357
The other side faces Nepal, near India.

13
00:01:06.678 --> 00:01:12.802
People in the Western world didn't really know much about Mount Everest until the 1920s.

14
00:01:13.803 --> 00:01:19.927
That's when British troops were sent to explore the Himalayas and discover a route to Everest's summit.

15
00:01:20.728 --> 00:01:26.412
But this expedition failed; and so did the next; and the next after that.

16
00:01:27.424 --> 00:01:36.990
Finally in 1953 Sir Edmund Hillary of New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay, a Nepalese guide, became the first

17
00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.691
people to stand on Mount Everest's peak.

18
00:01:40.672 --> 00:01:45.054
Since then over ten thousand people have tried to reach the summit.

19
00:01:45.995 --> 00:01:50.898
Thousands have succeeded, but hundreds of others have died while trying.

20
00:01:51.798 --> 00:01:58.430
Each of Mount Everest's three sides can be climbed, but the south side facing Nepal is the most

21
00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.311
popular. Mr.

22
00:02:00.391 --> 00:02:05.274
Fay said it usually takes 64 days to climb this side!

23
00:02:06.655 --> 00:02:09.616
An expedition on Mount Everest is hard work.

24
00:02:10.557 --> 00:02:18.201
Climbers have to travel over slippery rocks and sheets of ice as well as steep inclines and bottomless

25
00:02:18.302 --> 00:02:22.184
crevasses. Death is always possible.

26
00:02:23.088 --> 00:02:25.969
It takes a long time to prepare for a climb.

27
00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:32.332
For the first few days, climbers get ready in Kathmandu, the capital of Nepal.

28
00:02:33.312 --> 00:02:39.635
They meet the other members of their team, make sure they have everything they need, and hire porters

29
00:02:39.695 --> 00:02:41.196
to help carry the equipment.

30
00:02:42.176 --> 00:02:47.558
Most of the porters are Sherpa, Nepalese people who live in the valleys of the Himalayas.

31
00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:54.246
Without the help of the Sherpa and their yaks, most climbers wouldn't make it to the summit.

32
00:02:55.508 --> 00:03:03.174
When Sir Hillary climbed Mount Everest back in 1953, he said he felt like an ant in a

33
00:03:03.354 --> 00:03:04.475
giant's world.

34
00:03:05.476 --> 00:03:14.064
I can just imagine being surrounded by towering mounds of snow, ancient glaciers, and the majestic mountains of

35
00:03:14.084 --> 00:03:14.925
the Himalayas.

36
00:03:15.886 --> 00:03:18.228
That would make me feel pretty small too!

37
00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:26.322
Many people used to think that gods and demons lived in the mountains—along with a huge, ape-like monster

38
00:03:26.422 --> 00:03:28.463
called the Abominable Snowman.

39
00:03:29.323 --> 00:03:35.625
There are lots of stories about how giant footprints in the snow were made by the Abominable Snowman.

40
00:03:36.525 --> 00:03:38.986
Others say the footprints were made by bears.

41
00:03:39.866 --> 00:03:40.986
But who knows?

42
00:03:42.427 --> 00:03:48.857
The leading cause of death on Mount Everest is not the Abominable Snowman, but avalanches.

43
00:03:49.858 --> 00:03:56.922
Huge sections of the mountain can suddenly break off and slide down at speeds of over three hundred

44
00:03:56.982 --> 00:04:01.645
kilometers per hour, crushing and burying everything in their path!

45
00:04:02.626 --> 00:04:09.390
Also the glaciers that cover parts of the mountain sometimes move, causing deep cracks to form in the

46
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:17.609
ice. There are other dangers too, including low levels of oxygen that make climbers tired and dizzy.

47
00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:22.951
Frostbite occurs when fingers, toes, or facial parts freeze.

48
00:04:23.911 --> 00:04:30.313
In really serious cases, the frostbitten body parts have to be cut off—yikes!

49
00:04:31.473 --> 00:04:38.375
Some people are reminded of these dangers when they stumble across frozen dead bodies, the remains of other

50
00:04:38.435 --> 00:04:40.616
climbers who perished on Mount Everest.

51
00:04:42.064 --> 00:04:45.386
Reaching the summit takes a lot of courage and hard work.

52
00:04:46.207 --> 00:04:52.531
But for serious mountain climbers, there's no bigger dream than standing on top of Everest.

53
00:04:53.492 --> 00:04:57.014
Over the years climbers have done some incredible things.

54
00:04:57.975 --> 00:05:06.581
For example, in 1980 Reinhold Messner became the first man to climb to the peak completely alone.

55
00:05:07.601 --> 00:05:11.544
Several people have skied or snowboarded down from the summit.

56
00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:20.208
In 2001 a man named Erik Weihenmayer became the first blind man to stand on the top.

57
00:05:21.068 --> 00:05:22.229
Pretty awesome, huh?

58
00:05:23.109 --> 00:05:31.394
And in 2010 Jordan Romero became the youngest person to reach the summit—at thirteen years old!

59
00:05:32.294 --> 00:05:33.535
That's around my age!

60
00:05:34.515 --> 00:05:35.896
Now for the bad news.

61
00:05:36.848 --> 00:05:44.671
Unfortunately Mount Everest's popularity with climbers has taken its toll both on the slopes and in the valleys

62
00:05:44.771 --> 00:05:49.894
below. On the slopes, litter is a serious problem.

63
00:05:50.814 --> 00:05:56.597
It interferes with climbers' expeditions and it pollutes a really cool world landmark.

64
00:05:57.517 --> 00:06:05.381
There have been some major clean-up operations, and workers have brought back thousands of pounds of trash, including

65
00:06:05.641 --> 00:06:10.585
oxygen containers, tents, food wrappers, and human waste.

66
00:06:11.606 --> 00:06:17.712
The Nepalese side of Mount Everest and the valleys below it are part of Sagarmatha National Park.

67
00:06:18.572 --> 00:06:26.519
Because of deforestation, many animals in the valleys, such as snow leopards and Tibetan bears, have lost their

68
00:06:26.579 --> 00:06:34.273
homes. Massive numbers of trees are being cut down to provide enough firewood and construction material for all

69
00:06:34.313 --> 00:06:36.195
the climbers who pass through the region.

70
00:06:37.596 --> 00:06:40.419
Many people are aware of the deforestation issue.

71
00:06:41.339 --> 00:06:49.086
Local and international organizations are working hard to preserve and restore Mount Everest and its surrounding areas.

72
00:06:50.007 --> 00:06:56.092
For example, climbers are now encouraged to use kerosene instead of wood for fuel.

73
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:03.303
And each year over a million seedlings are planted in the valleys to replace those that were cut

74
00:07:03.364 --> 00:07:09.507
down. Organizations and workers are optimistic that the area will soon recover.

75
00:07:10.427 --> 00:07:13.989
Now I'll tell you why this lesson changed my life.

76
00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:22.574
I've found my life goal—to stand on top of the world someday, on the summit of Mount Everest!

77
00:07:23.674 --> 00:07:26.576
I guess I should start practicing my mountain climbing.

78
00:07:27.444 --> 00:07:29.786
Too bad there aren't many mountains around here!
